{"cuisine/caribbean-chinese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/caribbean-chinese-cuisine","n":"Caribbean Chinese cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Diaspora cuisine in China","intro":[["Caribbean Chinese cuisine is a style of food resulting from a fusion of Chinese and West Indian cuisines. The Chinese influence is predominantly Cantonese, the main source of Chinese immigrants to the West Indies. West Indian food is itself a mixture of West African, British, Indian-South Asian, Spanish, French, Dutch, Middle Eastern, and Indigenous cooking styles."],["Although a long-favoured cuisine in West Indian restaurants and Chinese Caribbean households, it is only recently that an increase in number of Caribbean–Chinese restaurants has occurred in Canada and the United States. These are more often than not \"Guyanese restaurants\" owing to that country's particular historical connection to Chinese immigration, although signs may also claim \"Caribbean Chinese food,\" \"West Indian and Chinese cuisine\", or variations thereof."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"caribbean-chinese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caribbean_Chinese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/alaska-native-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/alaska-native-foodways","n":"Alaska Native foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Alaska","intro":[["Alaska is home to many Native peoples, each with its own language and way of eating. The Alaska Native Heritage Center groups them into five regions: Iñupiaq and St. Lawrence Island Yupik in the north, Yup'ik and Cup'ik in the southwest, Unangax̂ and Alutiiq in the Aleutians and Southcentral, Athabascans in the Interior, and Eyak, Tlingit, Haida and Tsimshian in the Southeast."],["Food comes from the land and water close to home: ",["Salmon","ingredient/salmon"]," and other fish, ",["Seal oil","ingredient/seal-oil"]," and sea mammals on the coast, ",["Caribou","ingredient/reindeer"]," and ",["Moose","ingredient/moose"]," inland, and berries and greens picked in summer. Hunting, fishing, gathering and sharing with family and Elders are still at the center of how people eat today."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Alaska Native Heritage Center","url":"https://www.alaskanative.net/"},{"name":"University of Alaska Museum of the North, Alaska Native cultures guide","url":"https://uaf.edu/museum/education/docents/pdfs/NC2-3_Intro_2014.pdf"},{"name":"Alaska Native Tribal Health Consortium, Traditional Food Guide","url":"https://anthc.org/news/second-edition-of-traditional-food-guide-supports-modern-health"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"alaska-native-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":3,"rows":[{"name":"ANTHC healthy recipes","ref":"site/anthc-org","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"anthc.org","note":"Recipe cards from the Alaska Native Tribal Health Consortium, like seal posole.","url":"https://anthc.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/Recipe_Seal-Posole.pdf","suggested":true},{"name":"USDA FDPIR recipes and cookbooks","ref":"site/fna-usda-gov","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"fna.usda.gov","note":"Tribal cookbooks and Indigenous chefs' recipes gathered by the USDA.","url":"https://www.fna.usda.gov/fdpir/sharing-gallery/recipes-cookbooks","suggested":true},{"name":"NĀTIFS Indigenous Food Sovereignty recipes","ref":"site/natifs-org","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"natifs.org","note":"Recipes and videos from the Indigenous Food Lab and partner Indigenous chefs.","url":"https://natifs.org/indigenous-food-sovereignty-initiative-usda-fdpir/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Traditional Food Guide for Alaska Native People","ref":"cookbook/traditional-food-guide-for-alaska-native-people","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Alaska Native Tribal Health Consortium, 2015","note":"Wild meats, fish, seafood and plants from across Alaska, with Native names and preparation tips.","url":"https://anthc.org/news/second-edition-of-traditional-food-guide-supports-modern-health","suggested":true},{"name":"Nellie's Recipes: An Alaska Native Traditional Food Cookbook","ref":"cookbook/nellie-s-recipes-an-alaska-native-traditional-food-cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Alaska Native Tribal Health Consortium","note":"Family recipes like akutaq, salmon spread and Lower Yukon fry bread, written for Elders.","url":"https://anthc.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Nellie-final-2024.pdf","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Akutaq","dish/akutaq"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Animal fats and oils","ingredient/animal-fats-and-oils"],["Seal oil","ingredient/seal-oil"],["Berry","ingredient/berry"],["Whitefish","ingredient/whitefish"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Alaska Native cooking","count":10,"links":[["Alutiiq foodways","cuisine/alutiiq-foodways"],["Athabascan foodways","cuisine/athabascan-foodways"],["Eyak foodways","cuisine/eyak-foodways"],["Haida foodways","cuisine/haida-foodways"],["Iñupiaq foodways","cuisine/inupiaq-foodways"],["St. Lawrence Island Yupik foodways","cuisine/st-lawrence-island-yupik-foodways"],["Tlingit cuisine","cuisine/tlingit-cuisine"],["Tsimshian foodways","cuisine/tsimshian-foodways"],["Unangax̂ foodways","cuisine/unangax-foodways"],["Yup'ik cuisine","cuisine/yup-ik-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"ingredient/bay-leaf":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/bay-leaf","n":"Bay leaf","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 10 dishes across 9 cuisines","intro":[["Bay leaves are aromatic leaves commonly used as herbs in cooking many world cuisines; They come from various plants of species with distinctive flavours and fragrances as desired of those cuisines. The original English bay refers to the laurel (Laurus nobilis) common in Western especially Mediterranean cuisine. Other unrelated species yet dubbed as bay under similar applications include Umbellularia californica (\"California bay laurel\"), Cinnamomum tamala in Indian cuisine (hence \"Indian bay leaf\", or \"tejpat\"),  Syzygium polyanthum (\"Indonesian bay leaf\"),  Pimenta racemosa (\"West Indian bay\"), and Litsea glaucescens (\"Mexican bay leaf\")."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-bay-leaf"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bay_leaf"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":9,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Gudeg","dish/gudeg"],["Nasi liwet","dish/nasi-liwet"],["Rawon","dish/rawon"],["Saksang","dish/saksang"],["Tongseng","dish/tongseng"]]],["Belgian cuisine","cuisine/belgian-cuisine",[["Flemish stew","dish/flemish-stew"]]],["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine",[["Adobo (Filipino dish)","dish/adobo-filipino-dish"]]],["Hungarian cuisine","cuisine/hungarian-cuisine",[["Goulash","dish/goulash"]]],["Mindanao cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-mindanao",[["Adobo (Filipino dish)","dish/adobo-filipino-dish"]]],["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine",[["Bacalhau à Zé do Pipo","dish/bacalhau-a-ze-do-pipo"]]],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Goulash","dish/goulash"]]],["Viennese cuisine","cuisine/viennese-cuisine",[["Goulash","dish/goulash"]]],["Visayan cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-visayas",[["Adobo (Filipino dish)","dish/adobo-filipino-dish"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"],["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Red onion","ingredient/red-onion"],["Scallion","ingredient/scallion"]]}]},"ingredient/pandanus-tectorius":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/pandanus-tectorius","n":"Pandanus tectorius","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Pandanus tectorius is a species of Pandanus (screwpine) that is native to Malesia, Papuasia, eastern Australia, and the Pacific Islands. It grows in the coastal lowlands typically near the edge of the ocean. Common names in English include thatch screwpine, Tahitian screwpine, hala tree (pū hala in Hawaiian) and pandanus. The fruit is edible and sometimes known as hala fruit."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-pandanus-tectorius"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pandanus_tectorius"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Taiwan cuisine","cuisine/taiwan-cuisine",[["Alivongvong","dish/alivongvong"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]}]},"dish/bacalhau-a-gomes-de-sa":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/bacalhau-a-gomes-de-sa","n":"Bacalhau à Gomes de Sá","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Portuguese cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Bacalhau à Gomes de Sá is a common codfish (bacalhau) dish in Portugal, typical of the city of Porto."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-bacalhau-a-gomes-de-sa"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bacalhau_%C3%A0_Gomes_de_S%C3%A1"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":7,"links":[["Bacalhau","ingredient/bacalhau"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Olive","ingredient/olive"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Salt cod","ingredient/salt-cod"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Food From Portugal","ref":"site/foodfromportugal-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"foodfromportugal.com","note":"Portuguese-run recipe site and video channel of traditional Portuguese dishes.","url":"https://www.foodfromportugal.com/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Portuguese cuisine","links":[["Adega","dish/adega"],["Alheira","dish/alheira"],["Amêijoas à Bulhão Pato","dish/ameijoas-a-bulhao-pato"],["Arroz de marisco","dish/arroz-de-marisco"],["Bacalhau à Brás","dish/bacalhau-a-bras"],["Bacalhau à minhota","dish/bacalhau-a-minhota"],["Bacalhau à Narcisa","dish/bacalhau-a-narcisa"],["Bacalhau à Zé do Pipo","dish/bacalhau-a-ze-do-pipo"],["Bacalhau com natas","dish/bacalhau-com-natas"],["Batatas a murro","dish/batatas-a-murro"],["Bolinhos de bacalhau","dish/bolinhos-de-bacalhau"],["Bolo de bolacha","dish/bolo-de-bolacha"],["Canja de galinha","dish/canja-de-galinha"],["Cocido","dish/cocido"],["Embutido","dish/embutido"],["Encharcada","dish/encharcada"],["Fartura","dish/fartura"],["Feijoada","dish/feijoada"],["Fios de ovos","dish/fios-de-ovos"],["Francesinha","dish/francesinha"],["Gazpacho","dish/gazpacho"],["Pão Alentejano","dish/pao-alentejano"],["Pão de Ló","dish/pao-de-lo"],["Pataniscas","dish/pataniscas"],["Polvo à lagareiro","dish/polvo-a-lagareiro"],["Prego (Portuguese sandwich)","dish/prego-portuguese-sandwich"],["Pudim Abade de Priscos","dish/pudim-abade-de-priscos"],["Pudim Molotov","dish/pudim-molotov"],["Sericaia","dish/sericaia"],["Serradura","dish/serradura"],["Tocino de cielo","dish/tocino-de-cielo"]]}]},"ingredient/kosher-dill-pickle":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/kosher-dill-pickle","n":"Kosher dill pickle","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["A pickled cucumber – commonly known as a pickle in the United States and Canada and a gherkin ( GUR-kin) in Britain, Ireland, South Africa, Australia and New Zealand – is a usually small or miniature cucumber that has been pickled in a brine, vinegar, or other solution and left to ferment. The fermentation process is executed either by immersing the cucumbers in an acidic solution or through souring by lacto-fermentation. Pickled cucumbers are often part of mixed pickles."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-kosher-dill-pickle"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kosher_dill_pickle"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Celery salt","ingredient/celery-salt"],["Chicago-style relish","ingredient/chicago-style-relish"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Hot dog","ingredient/hot-dog"],["Mustard","ingredient/mustard"],["Mustard (condiment)","ingredient/mustard-condiment"],["Pickled cucumber","ingredient/pickled-cucumber"],["Poppy seed","ingredient/poppy-seed"],["Sport peppers","ingredient/sport-peppers"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Vienna Beef","ingredient/vienna-beef"],["Vienna sausage","ingredient/vienna-sausage"],["White onion","ingredient/white-onion"]]}]},"dish/demi-glace":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/demi-glace","n":"Demi-glace","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Demi-glace is a rich brown sauce in French cuisine used by itself or as a base for other sauces.  The term comes from the French word glace, which, when used in reference to a sauce, means \"icing\" or \"glaze\".  It is traditionally made by combining one part espagnole sauce and one part brown stock. The sauce is then reduced by half, strained of any leftover impurities, and finished with a sherry wine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-demi-glace"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demi-glace"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Red wine","ingredient/red-wine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pardon Your French","ref":"site/pardonyourfrench-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pardonyourfrench.com","note":"Audrey Le Goff, a French cook, shares classic and regional French home recipes.","url":"https://pardonyourfrench.com","suggested":true},{"name":"La Cuisine de Géraldine","ref":"site/lacuisinedegeraldine-fr","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lacuisinedegeraldine.fr","note":"French home cooking blog by Géraldine Leverd, also author of The New French Kitchen.","url":"https://lacuisinedegeraldine.fr/en/about-us","suggested":true},{"name":"New Caledonia Tourism: Kanak cooking","ref":"site/newcaledonia-travel","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"newcaledonia.travel","note":"Official tourism board overview of Kanak food, including bougna cooked in an earth oven.","url":"https://www.newcaledonia.travel/nz/gastronomy-wine/gastronomy/kanak-cooking","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from French cuisine","links":[["'t Fornuis","dish/t-fornuis"],["'t Misverstant","dish/t-misverstant"],["'t Vlasbloemeken","dish/t-vlasbloemeken"],["&moshik","dish/moshik"],["Aan de Poel","dish/aan-de-poel"],["Adrian (restaurant)","dish/adrian-restaurant"],["Adventureland Bazaar","dish/adventureland-bazaar"],["Alain Ducasse at The Dorchester","dish/alain-ducasse-at-the-dorchester"],["Albufera sauce","dish/albufera-sauce"],["Alex (restaurant)","dish/alex-restaurant"],["Andouillette","dish/andouillette"],["Arpège (restaurant)","dish/arpege-r